<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karachi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karachi is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> province, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> coast near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> delta. A small trading town in the 18th century, it grew under British rule from 1843 into a major port. It was the first capital of Pakistan from 1947 until the government moved north in 1959, and it remains the country's main commercial and financial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karachi.jpg" alt="Karachi" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ktownboy103 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clifton_Karachi_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karachi lies on the coastline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> province in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, along the Karachi Harbour, a natural harbour on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. It is built on a coastal plain with scattered rocky outcroppings, hills and marshlands, and mangrove forests grow in the brackish waters around the harbour and farther south-east towards the Indus River Delta. West of the city is Cape Monze, known locally as Ras Muari, an area of sea cliffs, sandstone promontories and beaches. Two small ranges, the Khasa Hills and the Mulri Hills, lie within the city in the north-west. They are barren, part of the larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirthar-mountains">Kirthar Range</a>, and reach a maximum height of 528 metres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region has been inhabited for millennia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-paleolithic">Upper Paleolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> sites have been excavated in the Mulri Hills on the northern outskirts, and their earliest inhabitants are believed to have been hunter-gatherers who left ancient flint tools. The region is believed to have been known to the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and may have been the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbarikon">Barbarikon</a>, an ancient seaport at the nearby mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. Karachi may also be the place called Ramya in ancient Greek texts.</p><p>The modern city was reputedly founded in 1729 as the fortified village of Kolachi-jo-Goth during the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhora-dynasty">Kalhora dynasty</a>, said to be named in honour of Mai Kolachi, whose son slew a man-eating crocodile. The shortened name Karachee was first used in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> report of 1742 about a shipwreck near the settlement. The village greatly increased in importance with the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in the mid-19th century, following the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. The colonial administration began substantial projects to turn it into a major seaport and to connect it to the railway network of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p><p>At independence in 1947 Karachi was the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, with an estimated 400,000 people and a slim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> majority. After the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, hundreds of thousands of Muslim refugees arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> while nearly all its Hindu residents left. Karachi served as the federal capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> from 1947 to 1959 and grew rapidly, attracting migrants from across the country and other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Karachi is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, with about 20 million people according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2023. It contains the country's two largest seaports, the Port of Karachi and Port Qasim, and its busiest airport, Jinnah International Airport. It is one of the most linguistically, ethnically and religiously diverse cities in Pakistan. Among its landmarks are Frere Hall, Empress Market and St. Patrick's Cathedral, which brought the first neo-Gothic buildings to the city, and Wazir Mansion, the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a>, now a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karachi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Britain took <b>Karachi</b> in 1839 and Sindh in 1843 and by 1893 ruled the whole region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the 19th century many printed editions (Pōthi Japji) appeared: at Lahore from 1865, in Gurmukhi and in Perso-Arabic script, some with commentaries by Bihārī Lāl and Maṇi Siṅgh; at Amritsar in 1875 and 1882; at <b>Karachi</b> in 1875 in the Khoja-Sindhi script; at Sialkot and at Rawalpindi.</blockquote><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a></li><li><blockquote>On 14 August 1947 the Dominion of Pakistan came into being, with Muhammad Ali Jinnah as Governor-General and <b>Karachi</b> as capital, comprising West Pakistan and East Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a></li></ul></section>
